{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1546886-outdated-technocracy-hypertech","id":1546886,"title":"\"Outdated\" Technocracy Hypertech","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-12-11T19:07:17-08:00","id":1546886,"title":"\"Outdated\" Technocracy Hypertech","content":"I'm trying to decide what happens to magickal hypertech when consensus &quot;advances&quot; past the point where what it does is no longer considered magickal.<br /> <br /> As an example, suppose an Electrodyne Engineer had put together an electric toaster a handful of years before consensus had embraced that use of electricity. Does it cease to be a Wonder and become a mundane antique toaster, or does it continue to be a Wonder with an entirely mundane effect and the very rare possibility it might do something bizarre when used?<br /> <br /> In one of the games I'm running, I'm anticipating the possibility that characters may gain access to a long-abandoned Order of Reason laboratory which was not properly decommissioned. (There is a reason why they sealed the place up without clearing it out first.) Aside from what they'd be after there, I'm trying to think of what other things they might find inside the place, and most of it would reasonably be information and projects which are rather dated.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28664-steve-bridger","name":"Steve Bridger","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-11T20:29:45-08:00","id":1546887,"title":"","content":"It's going to depend on how much it fits with modern Consensus. Much like plenty of Etherite Wonders, they might have a scientific flair, but they're still doing Science! not technology.<br /> <br /> And Order of Reason lab especially is likely going to still be Wonders because it'll be full of not just obsolete tech but pseudoscience that shouldn't work tech. The Order of Reason reorganized into the Technocracy in 1851. For example, this was a period where bloodletting was still a mainstream medical practice, and people were making &quot;better&quot; bloodletting machines. But in modern Consensus bloodletting is BS medical nonsense, so a Wonder that cures people by precisely targeting the proper blood to remove is going to be a Wonder to do anything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-11T20:34:30-08:00","id":1546889,"title":"","content":"Here's the progression I see: <br /> <br /> 1. A Wonder is created that counts as Vulgar. Think of it as an early prototype, highly prone to failure but still demonstrating a principle that the Technocracy's wants to incorporate into the Consensus. <br /> <br /> 2. The Device gets refined, and new models shift from embodying a (Vulgar) Procedure to a (Coincidental) Adjustment as there's growing acceptance of the underlying principle by the Consensus. They become more reliable as a result of this shift. <br /> <br /> 3. Once the Device is Coincidental, techno-sorcerers (&quot;talented engineers&quot;) start using the Path of Enchantment to produce a new generation of the Device that isn't even Coincidental; its operation is fully integrated into the Tapestry, not just the Consensus, even if its manufacturing process isn't. <br /> <br /> 4. Finally, the principles behind the Device become so ingrained in the Tapestry that it becomes possible to craft new devices without the need for the Path of Enchantment; you simply need the appropriate Attribute+Ability dice pool. The device is now mundane. This takes repetition over time, and cannot be rushed. <br /> <br /> Note that I describe that as four generations of devices. I would allow an early prototype to be rebuilt as a later generation of the technology; but barring that, the prototype would remain a prototype even as the underlying science becomes more firmly established. To answer Steve's question, I'd have the Order of Reason's lab contain stage 1, 2, and/or 3 technology as outlined above. Even though the scientific principles embodied in the technology have long since become mundane, these would be relics of a bygone age when those principles weren't as well understood as they are now, where the early designs were still flawed to some extent. They can be rebuilt to more modern specs; but until they're rebuilt, they remain what they were. <br /> <br /> Does that make sense?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-11T20:55:18-08:00","id":1546890,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1546886-outdated-technocracy-hypertech?p=1546887#post1546887\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's going to depend on how much it fits with modern Consensus. Much like plenty of Etherite Wonders, they might have a scientific flair, but they're still doing Science! not technology.<br /> <br /> And Order of Reason lab especially is likely going to still be Wonders because it'll be full of not just obsolete tech but pseudoscience that shouldn't work tech. The Order of Reason reorganized into the Technocracy in 1851. For example, this was a period where bloodletting was still a mainstream medical practice, and people were making &quot;better&quot; bloodletting machines. But in modern Consensus bloodletting is BS medical nonsense, so a Wonder that cures people by precisely targeting the proper blood to remove is going to be a Wonder to do anything.</div> </div> </div> </div><i>wr</i>In particular, the Void Engineers aren't all that inclined to operate within the modern Consensus: their Research and Execution Division is focused on getting technology (or, as the original <b>Technocracy: Void Engineers</b> book put it, Tecknology) out in the field ASAP: in the progression outlined in my last post, they focus heavily on stage one and two Devices, relying on the fact that in the Umbra (and even in Conventional Space far from Earth), you don't <i>need </i>\u200b\u200bto get the Consensus to accept the scientific principles. That's why you'll find force fields, laser swords, reactionless drives, faster than light propulsion, and all sorts of other technologies that we may never see on Earth, now or in the future. Conversely, they put <i>no </i>effort into advancing the technology to stages 3 or 4; they leave that entirely to the other Conventions. That simply doesn't fit their &quot;full speed ahead&quot; philosophy. <br /> <br /> And today's Tecknology stands a very real chance of becoming tomorrow's Ether Science as the underlying principles don't just fail to be accepted by the Consensus, but get outright rejected. That's the cost of pushing for a One Right Way To Do It: all of the other ways become Wrong.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-12T04:46:40-08:00","id":1546898,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1546886-outdated-technocracy-hypertech?p=1546889#post1546889\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here's the progression I see:<br /> <br /> 1. A Wonder is created that counts as Vulgar. Think of it as an early prototype, highly prone to failure but still demonstrating a principle that the Technocracy's wants to incorporate into the Consensus.<br /> <br /> 2. The Device gets refined, and new models shift from embodying a (Vulgar) Procedure to a (Coincidental) Adjustment as there's growing acceptance of the underlying principle by the Consensus. They become more reliable as a result of this shift.<br /> <br /> 3. Once the Device is Coincidental, techno-sorcerers (&quot;talented engineers&quot;) start using the Path of Enchantment to produce a new generation of the Device that isn't even Coincidental; its operation is fully integrated into the Tapestry, not just the Consensus, even if its manufacturing process isn't.<br /> <br /> 4. Finally, the principles behind the Device become so ingrained in the Tapestry that it becomes possible to craft new devices without the need for the Path of Enchantment; you simply need the appropriate Attribute+Ability dice pool. The device is now mundane. This takes repetition over time, and cannot be rushed.<br /> <br /> Note that I describe that as four generations of devices. I would allow an early prototype to be rebuilt as a later generation of the technology; but barring that, the prototype would remain a prototype even as the underlying science becomes more firmly established. To answer Steve's question, I'd have the Order of Reason's lab contain stage 1, 2, and/or 3 technology as outlined above. Even though the scientific principles embodied in the technology have long since become mundane, these would be relics of a bygone age when those principles weren't as well understood as they are now, where the early designs were still flawed to some extent. They can be rebuilt to more modern specs; but until they're rebuilt, they remain what they were.<br /> <br /> Does that make sense?</div> </div> </div> </div>That seems like a perfectly logical progression to me. Provided that the characters do decide to seek the place out, and are able to effectively deal with a certain problem therein, I'll give them a mix of things to investigate (beyond their primary reason for being there in the first place.) Mainly notes and prototypes involving things which hadn't quite been invented or accepted yet, according to the consensus of the time. Nothing terribly useful, but fascinating in a historical sense.<br /> <br /> One of the characters is an Etherite who'll likely have a field day going over it all, as he's the sort who tends to work within or very close to consensus a lot of the time. He has the skills and background to deal with many things using conventional science, but he's not afraid to throw in a little dash of Science! when inspiration hits him, at least when he's working inside his own Sanctum/Laboratory or circumstances necessitate a slightly unorthodox approach. (The player likes to characterize him as a rather deadpan-stuffy researcher most of the time, but he occasionally gets &quot;that look&quot; when he's about to do something a bit out of the ordinary..)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28664,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28664-steve-bridger","user":"Steve Bridger","date":". 12-12-2024, 07:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28664-steve-bridger","name":"Steve Bridger","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-12T18:51:22-08:00","id":1546911,"title":"","content":"A Smartphone in 1940's would be wonders.<br /> <br /> And there's a good chance that the Union had been using those at that time. <br /> <br /> After 2010 thou, any one of those WWII relics would be mundane, except if some sleeper research were to carbon date one of them. Then, it would be room 101 for him<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-13T02:06:17-08:00","id":1546919,"title":"","content":"I would imagine that the Technocracy makes a point of <i>not </i>keeping these sorts of things around, and that even those examples on display inside Constructs are probably mock-ups or just the shell of the original. Still, there's probably the odd Technocrat here and there who has secretly kept their old Vacuum Cleaner of the Future prototype out of nostalgia, or some higher up who stubbornly refuses to trade in their groovy looking desk videophone (and has enough clout to shoo off underlings who insist that it needs to be replaced.)<br /> <br /> I suppose I could consider these things to still be Wonders of one sort or another, but Consensus doesn't react very strongly at all when they &quot;malfunction&quot; during use, and any resulting Paradox just instantly manifests as harmlessly mundane (though possibly humorous) technical or mechanical hiccups. The reason why the Technocracy doesn't likely want these sorts of things laying around is that a Sleeper engineer might find little things which don't quite make sense, if they were to start examining them very closely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28664,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28664-steve-bridger","user":"Steve Bridger","date":". 12-17-2024, 08:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28664-steve-bridger","name":"Steve Bridger","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-15T13:46:13-08:00","id":1547041,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Steve Bridger</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1546886-outdated-technocracy-hypertech?p=1546919#post1546919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would imagine that the Technocracy makes a point of <i>not </i>keeping these sorts of things around, and that even those examples on display inside Constructs are probably mock-ups or just the shell of the original. Still, there's probably the odd Technocrat here and there who has secretly kept their old Vacuum Cleaner of the Future prototype out of nostalgia, or some higher up who stubbornly refuses to trade in their groovy looking desk videophone (and has enough clout to shoo off underlings who insist that it needs to be replaced.)<br /> <br /> I suppose I could consider these things to still be Wonders of one sort or another, but Consensus doesn't react very strongly at all when they &quot;malfunction&quot; during use, and any resulting Paradox just instantly manifests as harmlessly mundane (though possibly humorous) technical or mechanical hiccups. The reason why the Technocracy doesn't likely want these sorts of thing laying around is that a Sleeper engineer might find little things which don't quite make sense, if they were start examining them very closely.</div> </div> </div> </div>Another reason for the Techies to recycle the technologies of the future from yesterday, is because after they become mundane, they would basically be free Quintessence in the form of Tass. Wonders do have quite a substantial amount of Quintessence into them after all, and the Union isn't the sort of organization that just simply let resources rot due to emotional attachment","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-15T15:44:10-08:00","id":1547045,"title":"","content":"One thing to consider is that Wonders that were Vulgar once upon a time may remain Vulgar, simply because there's a Consensus about what was technologically possible at a given time. If a Hypothetical Average Observer sees a 1940s smartphone, even if smartphones are currently incorporated into the Tapestry, they're going to think &quot;that's impossible, that couldn't exist then&quot;. (Mostly; we're all becoming increasingly aware of how more people in Real Life are starting to believe conspiratorial nonsense, including insisting ancient wonders were evidence of technology from an advanced and/or alien civilization).<br /> <br /> A Wonder can remain Vulgar simply because nothing <i>that old</i> should be <i>that advanced</i>. Or at least that's how the lay public feels about it, mostly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-16T02:52:37-08:00","id":1547051,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1546886-outdated-technocracy-hypertech?p=1547045#post1547045\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing to consider is that Wonders that were Vulgar once upon a time may remain Vulgar, simply because there's a Consensus about what was technologically possible at a given time. If a Hypothetical Average Observer sees a 1940s smartphone, even if smartphones are currently incorporated into the Tapestry, they're going to think &quot;that's impossible, that couldn't exist then&quot;. (Mostly; we're all becoming increasingly aware of how more people in Real Life are starting to believe conspiratorial nonsense, including insisting ancient wonders were evidence of technology from an advanced and/or alien civilization).<br /> <br /> A Wonder can remain Vulgar simply because nothing <i>that old</i> should be <i>that advanced</i>. Or at least that's how the lay public feels about it, mostly.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, which is why a smartphone from the 1940's should be absolutely mundane, until a sleeper carbon date it, upon which it becomes immediately vulgar.<br /> <br /> As I subscribe to the &quot;Omniscient Observer&quot; however, yes, I'd agree that is a vulgar wonder, thus &quot;recycling&quot; it is the best that can be done","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-16T06:07:11-08:00","id":1547055,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1546886-outdated-technocracy-hypertech?p=1547045#post1547045\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing to consider is that Wonders that were Vulgar once upon a time may remain Vulgar, simply because there's a Consensus about what was technologically possible at a given time. If a Hypothetical Average Observer sees a 1940s smartphone, even if smartphones are currently incorporated into the Tapestry, they're going to think &quot;that's impossible, that couldn't exist then&quot;. (Mostly; we're all becoming increasingly aware of how more people in Real Life are starting to believe conspiratorial nonsense, including insisting ancient wonders were evidence of technology from an advanced and/or alien civilization).<br /> <br /> A Wonder can remain Vulgar simply because nothing <i>that old</i> should be <i>that advanced</i>. Or at least that's how the lay public feels about it, mostly.</div> </div> </div> </div>That could be true in certain cases where the inner workings of such a thing would be visible to the average observer, and what it does wouldn't totally make sense in obvious view of that. On the other hand, if the inner workings are not casually obvious, making very modern technology look stylistically retro is a thing anyone even passingly aware of steampunk culture would be familiar with. If the Hypothetical Average Observer would be more likely to say &quot;Hah, that's amusing!' than &quot;Bloody hell, that's impossible!&quot; I would consider it coincidental.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28664-steve-bridger","name":"Steve Bridger","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-16T14:45:11-08:00","id":1547074,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Steve Bridger</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1546886-outdated-technocracy-hypertech?p=1547055#post1547055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...stylistically retro...</div> </div> </div> </div>This is something that's ultimately going to be a case-by-case call best made at the table, but it is important to remember that &quot;stylistically retro&quot; is exceedingly different from what stuff actually looked like in the past, and even more so is generally very different from what was &quot;stylistically futuristic&quot; back then. What a Technocrat in the 1940s would do to make a futuristic communications device would look nothing like what a 2020s Etherite hipster would make to do a retro smartphone.<br /> <br /> Where that line is, is wildly blurry. &quot;Average&quot; gets very subjective when we're talking about human perceptions. Given, for example, the large number of WWII era films and video games out there, I would say a lot of people (maybe average?) know what a combat 40s portable radio looks like because they're in pretty much every one of those pieces of media. But that was potentially less true in the 90s when prop and video-game realism was much lower so most people (considering most people in the 90s where born after WWII) would only know what one looks like if they went to a museum or something similar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-16T19:47:30-08:00","id":1547085,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1546886-outdated-technocracy-hypertech?p=1547074#post1547074\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is something that's ultimately going to be a case-by-case call best made at the table, but it is important to remember that &quot;stylistically retro&quot; is exceedingly different from what stuff actually looked like in the past, and even more so is generally very different from what was &quot;stylistically futuristic&quot; back then. What a Technocrat in the 1940s would do to make a futuristic communications device would look nothing like what a 2020s Etherite hipster would make to do a retro smartphone.<br /> <br /> Where that line is, is wildly blurry. &quot;Average&quot; gets very subjective when we're talking about human perceptions. Given, for example, the large number of WWII era films and video games out there, I would say a lot of people (maybe average?) know what a combat 40s portable radio looks like because they're in pretty much every one of those pieces of media. But that was potentially less true in the 90s when prop and video-game realism was much lower so most people (considering most people in the 90s where born after WWII) would only know what one looks like if they went to a museum or something similar.</div> </div> </div> </div>We've dealt with this sort of thing in the past, mostly in relation to a couple people gleefully playing really wacky Etherite characters. Generally speaking, we've assumed that Consensus or the Hypothetical Average Observer cannot see what's inside Etherite &quot;black boxes&quot; any more than it can see the contents of people's pockets. If that &quot;black box&quot; <i>could </i>potentially contain a mechanism which would be able to produce a particular effect without snagging the &quot;that's impossible/it's a miracle&quot; tripwire, then we're content to call that effect coincidental <i>unless </i>a Sleeper actually looks inside the box. The &quot;black box&quot; might be odd looking, but what it seems to be doing is generally not going to inspire outright disbelief.<br /> <br /> Naturally, this assumes a Hypothetical Average Observer, rather than a Hypothetical Omniscient Observer. I gave the HOO the boot some fifteen years ago, around the same time I stopped trying to sic Paradox on every magickal effect I found inconvenient to a plotline, and was inspired to write a Usenet article titled &quot;How I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love Time and Correspondence.&quot; (The amount of vitriol which was hurled at the latter gives me a chuckle to this day.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28664,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28664-steve-bridger","user":"Steve Bridger","date":". 12-16-2024, 11:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28664-steve-bridger","name":"Steve Bridger","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-16T21:15:27-08:00","id":1547091,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Steve Bridger</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1546886-outdated-technocracy-hypertech?p=1547085#post1547085\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> We've dealt with this sort of thing in the past, mostly in relation to a couple people gleefully playing really wacky Etherite characters. Generally speaking, we've assumed that Consensus or the Hypothetical Average Observer cannot see what's inside Etherite &quot;black boxes&quot; any more than it can see the contents of people's pockets. If that &quot;black box&quot; <i>could </i>potentially contain a mechanism which would be able to produce a particular effect without snagging the &quot;that's impossible/it's a miracle&quot; tripwire, then we're content to call that effect coincidental <i>unless </i>a Sleeper actually looks inside the box. The &quot;black box&quot; might be odd looking, but what it seems to be doing is generally not going to inspire outright disbelief.<br /> <br /> Naturally, this assumes a Hypothetical Average Observer, rather than a Hypothetical Omniscient Observer. I gave the HOO the boot some fifteen years ago, around the same time I stopped trying to sic Paradox on every magickal effect I found inconvenient to a plotline, and was inspired to write a Usenet article titled &quot;How I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love Time and Correspondence.&quot; (The amount of vitriol which was hurled at the latter gives me a chuckle to this day.)</div> </div> </div> </div>I hate the Hypothetical Average Observer. I only use the Omniscient Observer. Using the HAO implies that the universe can be tricked if you use a curtain in front of your effects, which is stupid. It turns pieces of cloth into the most powerful items of the universe. But that's just me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-17T08:25:49-08:00","id":1547107,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1546886-outdated-technocracy-hypertech?p=1547091#post1547091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I hate the Hypothetical Average Observer. I only use the Omniscient Observer. Using the HAO implies that the universe can be tricked if you use a curtain in front of your effects, which is stupid. It turns pieces of cloth into the most powerful items of the universe. But that's just me.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be honest, trying to use Paradox (or the threat of Paradox) to limit the decisions of the players in either of my current Mage games would be borderline useless anyhow, even if I still cared to try doing that. 50% of the time their characters get things done without resorting to magick at all. 40% of the time they're using magick to enhance or augment the application of non-magickal abilities, in ways which are so coincidental that near anyone would start remarking on the smell of cheese were I to try arguing with it. Most of these people have over a decade of regular experience when it comes to producing highly effective magickal results in ways which widely dodge vulgarity, and I've long since learned to be extremely flexible with my plotlines and just go with it. As a Mage Storyteller, it's not rules lawyers you really have to be concerned about, if you want to try running linear storylines (which I no longer even attempt.) It's players who're clever enough to realize that a little bit of magick can go a long, long way when applied with a good bit of judicious forethought.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28664-steve-bridger","name":"Steve Bridger","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}